.TH LIL 1
.SH NAME
lil \- little Limbo: an interpreted subset, in place of a shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lil
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lil
reads statements in a small subset of Limbo and runs them as they are
typed.
It exists for boards where a shell does not fit \- chiefly
.IR stm32f407 ,
which has 147456 bytes for the Dis pool and whose
.I mkfile
records that
.B /dis/sh.dis
asks for 73536 of them for its instruction array alone.
.PP
The size of the shell is not the reason, though.  Execution-in-place
puts an instruction array in flash, which is the only reason a shell
runs on that board at all, and it would do the same for a smaller one.
What XIP does not help with is the shell's semantics: a command is a
process and a redirection is an open, so
.PP
.EX
	echo rssioffset -90 >/dev/radio0/ctl
.EE
.PP
forks twice to write eleven bytes.
.PP
That used to kill the board.  It does not any more, and the honest
version of this page has to say so, because it was the argument
.I lil
was built on.  The board's configuration file records
.B "no procs for kproc"
against exactly that command, and
.B tools/radio/board.py
records the same failure from the other side \- a monitor running one
probe every two seconds killing the shell in a few minutes \- but both
predate
.B KSTACKPREALLOC
in
.BR mem.h ,
which takes every kernel stack in one piece at boot so a fork cannot
fail for want of one.  Measured on the current kernel by
.BR tools/radio/lilvsh.sh ,
ninety of those redirections two seconds apart leave the shell alive with
its live heap unmoved, 358 objects before and 363 after.  Forking at that
rate is no longer the limit.
.PP
What remains is smaller and measured.  Those ninety redirections cost
12665 heap allocations, 141 apiece; the same ninety writes through one
descriptor held in a
.I lil
variable cost 4610, or 51.  And two things still kill the shell outright,
for which see
.B "WHAT THE BOARD CANNOT DO"
below.
.PP
Those two numbers are the model's.  On silicon \- an stm32f407 with the
pool it really has, the same board and the same console for both, forty
writes each way with
.B /dev/memory
read before and after:
.PP
.EX
	                     allocations   per write   live heap
	sh   echo ... >ctl         +5856       146.4          +1
	lil  one descriptor         +340         8.5           0
.EE
.PP
Seventeen to one, and
.IR lil 's
figure is the pessimistic one: each of its forty writes had a
.B print
of a liveness marker between them, forty extra statements the shell's
forty redirections cannot have at all.  The shell's 141 carried over
almost exactly;
.IR lil 's
51 did not, and is six times better on the board than on the model.
.PP
And one thing silicon says that the model did not.  Typed at a console
that had been in use for a long session, the shell could not load
.I lil
at all \-
.PP
.EX
	; lil
	[Sh] Broken: "out of memory: heap" at 0x0 pid 4
.EE
.PP
which killed the shell and left the console undrained, the state that
ends at the reset button.  From a fresh boot, with 38109 bytes free in
the heap pool at the prompt, the same command works.  So the ceiling
this page was written around is real; it is the shell's accumulated heap
and not
.IR lil 's
size.
.PP
What is wanted instead is a language, in which a file is opened once,
kept in a variable, and written to as often as one likes:
.PP
.EX
	ctl := open("/dev/radio0/ctl", "w")
	write(ctl, "freq 915000000")
	write(ctl, "rate 9600")
	print(slurp("/dev/radio0/stats"))
.EE
.PP
The syntax is Limbo's because that is the language this tree is written
in, so that what is typed at a console and what is compiled into a
.B .dis
look alike.  A Forth would have been smaller and a Tcl more uniform;
both would have been one more language to know for no gain.
.SS Statements
.TP
.BI x " := e"
Introduce
.I x
and give it the value of
.IR e .
.TP
.BI x " = e"
Assign to an
.I x
that already exists.  A name that does not is an error and not a new
variable: a mistyped name on the left of
.B =
otherwise makes a variable nobody reads, and the write goes nowhere,
silently, which is the one class of mistake a console session cannot
see.
.TP
.BI if( e ) { ... } " else " { ... }
The
.B else
must begin on the same line as the closing brace of the
.B if
body.  A line is read as complete once its brackets balance, and there
is nothing to look ahead with.
.TP
.BI while( e ) { ... }
Bounded by time, not by a count of iterations: see
.B timelimit
below.
.PP
Statements are separated by
.B ;
or by a newline, and a line whose brackets do not balance is continued,
so a loop can be typed over several lines.
.B #
begins a comment.
.SS Values
There are three kinds: an integer, a string, and an open file.
That is deliberate and not a stage on the way to more.  Holding a file
descriptor across statements is the whole purpose; a program that wants
lists, tuples, ADTs or functions of its own should be compiled.
Integers may be written in decimal, or in hex as
.B 0x0a
or
.BR 16r0a ,
because every register and sync word on this bench is written that way.
.B +
on a string concatenates, and comparisons work on both kinds.
.SS Builtins
.TP
.BI open( path [, mode ])
.I Mode
is
.BR r ,
.B w
or
.BR rw ,
and defaults to
.BR r .
.TP
.BI create( path )
.TP
.BI read( fd [, n ])
One read of at most
.I n
bytes, 256 by default.  This is what a device file wants: a Styx read
boundary is a message boundary, so a short read is a whole answer.
.TP
.BI readall( fd )
Read until end of file.  A device file need never give one.
.TP
.BI slurp( path )
Open, read to end of file, and close.  This is
.IR cat (1),
and it is most of what the shell was used for here.
.TP
.BI write( fd , " e " ...)
Write the concatenation of its arguments, with no newline added.  One
call is one write, and so one Styx message.
.TP
.BI close( x )
.I X
must be the name of a variable.  Limbo shuts a file when the last
reference to it goes, and the reference that matters is the one in the
variable table, so this is a statement rather than a builtin: written as
a builtin it was handed the value and not the name, and reported success
while closing nothing.
.TP
.BI dir( path )
The names in a directory, one to a line, with
.B /
after those that are themselves directories.
.TP
.BI bind( from , " to" [, " flag" ])
.I Flag
is
.BR a ,
.B b
or
.BR c ,
for after, before and create; replace is the default.
.TP
.BI sleep( ms )
.TP
.B millisec()
.TP
.BI len( e )
.TP
.BI timelimit([ ms ])
How long one
.B while
may run, 60000 by default; returns the bound.
.B timelimit(0)
removes it, and on silicon that should be thought about first: with no
bound and no interrupt character, a mistyped condition costs the reset
button.  It is right for a monitor meant to run and wrong for anything
typed by hand.
.PP
The bound is in time and not in iterations because a count is not a
timeout: what matters is how long the console goes undrained.  This
board's
.IR devcons (3)
has no interrupt character \- nothing in
.B kbdputc()
turns a keystroke into a note \- so a loop that does not end can only be
left by resetting the board, and a mistyped condition should not cost a
power cycle.  When the bound trips it says so, rather than quietly
returning a half-finished loop.
.SS What it does not have
No functions of one's own, no lists, tuples or declared types, no
.BR for ,
no
.B &&
or
.BR || ,
no
.B ~
or regular expressions, no globbing, no pipes and no redirection.  The
last three are absent on purpose: they are the shell semantics this
exists to avoid.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/lil.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR limbo (1)
.SH BUGS
There is no syntax tree.  A
.B while
remembers the source text of its condition and body and re-scans them
each time round, which is slow; it is the trade a Forth makes too, on a
machine where a human types the program one line at a time.
.PP
.B print
is the only way to see a value; there is no automatic printing of a bare
expression.
.SH VERIFIED
What has actually been run, and what has not.
.PP
Under the host
.IR emu (1)
on a 64-bit Dis tree: every statement form and every builtin \-
declaration and assignment, hex and negative literals, string and
integer comparison,
.B if
with and without
.BR else ,
.B while
including its time bound tripping and being raised, a
.B create
written to twice through one held-open descriptor and read back, and
.B close
followed by a
.B read
that correctly fails.  The module compiles clean with warnings enabled at
both Dis word sizes and is 12447 bytes at
.BR DISWORD=4 ,
against 41629 for
.BR sh.dis .
.PP
That its instruction array is in flash rather than in the pool has been
checked and not inferred from its being in
.BR XIPDIS .
It is
.B "RODATA $24768"
in
.BR stm32f407.prog.s ,
and typing
.B lil
at a prompt moves the Dis heap from 39936 bytes to 50240 \- 10304 for the
module's data, its type descriptors and its own buffers, with the 24768
coming from flash.  Had the delta been 24768 the size argument here would
have been wrong.  For comparison in the same file,
.BR sh.dis 's
array is 73332 bytes, which agrees with the 73536 the board's
.I mkfile
has recorded all along, and
.BR ls.dis 's
is 6324 \- so when
.I ls
dies for want of memory on this board, its code is not what is too big.
.PP
On the
.I stm32f407
kernel under
.IR qemu (1),
which is the machine it is for, by
.BR tools/radio/liltest.sh :
31 cases, all passing.  Every statement form and every builtin that this
board can reach, four negative controls \- an
.B else
after a taken
.BR if ,
both untaken branches, and a
.B read
through a closed descriptor \- and three errors that must be reported
rather than swallowed.  A case that prints nothing counts as a failure,
so a hang or a crash cannot read as a pass.
.PP
.B create
and a file written and read back are
.I not
covered there, because this board has nowhere to write: its root is
devroot, served read-only out of flash, and nothing binds a ram file
system over it.  The shell gets
.B "permission denied"
for a redirection into
.BR /tmp .
Those paths are covered under the host
.I emu
instead, and
.B write
is exercised on the board against the radio's ctl file, which is the
write that matters here anyway.
.PP
It loads from flash, prints its banner and runs statements.  Its allocation is flat \- twenty-iteration loops
run four times over, with
.B /dev/memory
read between them, left the heap's cursize unmoved at 50048 bytes and its
live count at exactly 459, allocations and frees tracking each other
exactly (538/79, 885/426, 1232/773, 1579/1120).  So the interpreter
holds nothing between statements.
.PP
The comparison against the shell has been made, by
.BR tools/radio/lilvsh.sh ,
and it went against the claim it was written to test: ninety writes of
.B "rssioffset -90"
two seconds apart, paced one character at a time, and
.I both
survived.  The shell went from 358 live objects to 363 over 12665
allocations;
.I lil
went from 455 to 462 over 4610.  So at that rate forking is not the limit
and this measurement does not justify
.IR lil ;
what it does establish is that neither leaks and that
.I lil
allocates about a third as much per ctl write.  On the board that ratio
is seventeen to one rather than three; see above.
.PP
.B "And it has now run on silicon,"
by
.BR tools/radio/lilsilicon.py ,
on an stm32f407 with the Dis pool it really has and over its own uart
rather than a pty.  From a fresh boot: it loads, it answers a marker in
its own language, it opens the radio's ctl file into a variable, and it
takes forty writes through that one descriptor with a
.B print
of a marker between each \- forty of forty markers came back twice while
the file stayed open, which is the thing a shell's
.B "cat >file"
structurally cannot do.
.PP
Then
.B print(slurp("/dev/radio0/stats"))
read
.B "rssi -89 offset -90"
off the part, and the offset is the check that the forty writes were not
merely accepted: it boots at -102.
.B dir("/dis")
returned, and
.I lil
still had its prompt afterwards.
.PP
Its allocation on the board is flat, as it was on the model: over those
forty writes the heap's cursize did not move, allocations and frees were
both +340, and the live count came out unchanged.
.SH "A MISREADING, CORRECTED\"
An earlier version of this page and of the commit that added
.I lil
said that every Dis heap allocation in this tree is never reclaimed, on
the evidence of a compiled Limbo loop whose body was only
.B "t := s[1:]"
growing a column of
.B /dev/memory
by one per iteration.  That was wrong, and wrong in the ordinary way: the
column counts allocations made, not objects alive, and the column beside
it counts frees.  On the board the two track each other exactly and the
live figure does not move.
.PP
What is true is narrower and still worth knowing.  Under the host
.I emu
the same loop freed 15 times in 20000 allocations, grew the arena to 25
megabytes and died at
.BR "out of memory: heap" ,
having extended the arena rather than collected.  The board's kernel does
not behave that way, so this is
.IR emu 's
own allocator and not the interpreter's, and a memory measurement taken
under the host
.I emu
should not be believed about the board.
.SH "WHAT STILL KILLS THE SHELL AND DOES NOT KILL THIS"
Two things, both measured on the current kernel, and they are what
.I lil
is now justified by rather than the cost of forking.
.PP
A directory read.
.B "ls /dis"
gives
.BR "out of memory: heap" ,
and
.B "ls /dev"
attempted after it kills the shell outright with
.BR "[Sh] Broken" ,
after which nothing drains the console queue and every character
subsequently typed allocates a block nobody frees.
.I Lil
reaches the same ceiling \-
.B sys->dirread
on
.B /dis
wants more than the 40310 bytes free at a prompt, which is the board's
limit and not the interpreter's \- but it reports the failure and keeps
its prompt.
.PP
And a held-open file.  To stop forking,
.B board.py
holds the ctl file open with
.BR "cat >file" ,
and its own comment explains what that costs: inside that
.I cat
a liveness marker cannot be sent, because it would be written to the ctl
file rather than run.  So the only way to stop forking was to give up
being able to ask whether the board was still alive, and everything
downstream of that \- inferring liveness from whether a reply repeated,
counting echoes that keep coming back from the uart interrupt handler
long after the shell is dead \- exists to guess at what a marker would
have said outright.  In
.I lil
the file is a variable and writing to it is an ordinary statement, so a
marker is just another statement, and
.B board.py 's
.B LilStream.alive()
asks the question that
.B Stream
structurally cannot, and
.B tools/radio/lilstream.py
shows it doing so against a booted kernel: a marker answered with the ctl
file open, three ctl lines written through it, a marker answered again
afterwards, the part read back through
.B slurp
to confirm the writes landed, and a usable shell prompt after leaving.
Eight cases, all passing, with no board \-
.I qemu
is given
.B "-serial pty"
and
.B board.py
aimed at that path by
.BR INFERNO_BOARD_PORT ,
which also stops it looking for a
.IR screen (1)
session, since a test that found the bench's screen instead would type at
the physical board.
.PP
A third, smaller: a character dropped because the line was typed faster
than the console echoes leaves
.I lil
on its continuation prompt, which says what happened.  The shell gives a
parse error.
.SH "WHAT THE BOARD CANNOT DO"
.B dir
on a large directory exhausts the Dis heap.  The board has about 40310
bytes free at a prompt, and
.B sys->dirread
on
.B /dis
wants more than that before
.I lil
has printed a name.
.PP
This is the board and not
.IR lil .
The same kernel fails the same way under the shell:
.B "ls /dis"
gives
.BR "out of memory: heap" ,
and
.B "ls /dev"
after it kills the shell outright with
.BR "[Sh] Broken" .
The board's own configuration file has recorded the marginal case for
some time \-
.B "ls /dis | wc -l"
is written there as what killed the shell.
.PP
The difference worth noting is what happens next.
.I Lil
reported the failure and kept its prompt; the shell died and took the
console with it, and nothing then drains the console queue.
